        "question": {
            "id": 19,
            "category": "debugging",
            "intent": "parallel_subgraph_state_collision",
            "difficulty": "very_hard",
            "question": "A LangGraph workflow executes a subgraph and a normal parent-graph node in parallel after the same upstream node. The subgraph writes one state key while the parent node writes a different state key, yet execution fails with InvalidUpdateError saying that another state key can receive only one value per step. Review the implementation and explain how LangGraph applies writes from parallel tasks, how channels determine whether multiple values are allowed, why a subgraph can participate in a concurrent update even when it does not directly modify the reported key, and under what conditions an Annotated reducer is actually required. Distinguish the logical state keys written by individual nodes from the channel-level writes accumulated by the Pregel execution step.",
            "expected_files": [
                "errors/INVALID_CONCURRENT_GRAPH_UPDATE.mdx",
                "graph-api.mdx",
                "pregel.mdx",
                "langgraph/langgraph/channels/base.py",
                "langgraph/langgraph/channels/last_value.py",
                "langgraph/langgraph/channels/binop.py",
                "langgraph/langgraph/pregel/main.py",
                "langgraph/langgraph/graph/state.py"
            ],
            "concepts": [
                "Pregel execution",
                "parallel writes",
                "channels",
                "LastValue",
                "reducers",
                "Annotated state",
                "subgraphs",
                "concurrent graph updates"
            ],
            "tags": [
                "parallel_execution",
                "subgraph_state",
                "invalid_concurrent_graph_update",
                "last_value",
                "reducers",
                "pregel_writes"
            ],
            "code_snippet": "from typing import TypedDict\nfrom langgraph.graph import StateGraph, START, END\n\nclass State(TypedDict):\n    a: str\n    b: str\n\ndef parent_node(state):\n    return {\"b\": \"parent-result\"}\n\ndef subgraph_node(state):\n    return {\"a\": \"subgraph-result\"}\n\nchild = StateGraph(State)\nchild.add_node(\"child\", subgraph_node)\nchild.add_edge(START, \"child\")\nchild.add_edge(\"child\", END)\nsubgraph = child.compile()\n\nbuilder = StateGraph(State)\nbuilder.add_node(\"parent\", parent_node)\nbuilder.add_node(\"subgraph\", subgraph)\nbuilder.add_edge(START, \"parent\")\nbuilder.add_edge(START, \"subgraph\")\nbuilder.add_edge(\"parent\", END)\nbuilder.add_edge(\"subgraph\", END)\n\ngraph = builder.compile()\ngraph.invoke({\"a\": \"\", \"b\": \"\"})",
            "observed_behavior": "A state update can fail with a concurrent-update error even though the application believes the parallel tasks are updating independent keys.",
            "expected_behavior": "The application should understand which channel receives the conflicting writes, why the Pregel step considers them concurrent, and define an appropriate reducer only when the state semantics genuinely allow multiple values.",
            "error": "langgraph.errors.InvalidUpdateError: At key 'b': Can receive only one value per step. Use an Annotated key to handle multiple values.",
            "logs": null,
            "evidence": null,
            "queries": [
                "How does LangGraph apply writes from parallel nodes and subgraphs?",
                "Why can a parallel subgraph cause INVALID_CONCURRENT_GRAPH_UPDATE for a state key it does not explicitly modify?",
                "How do LangGraph channels determine whether multiple values can be written in one step?",
                "How does LastValue behave when multiple tasks write during the same Pregel step?",
                "When does LangGraph require an Annotated reducer for concurrent state updates?",
                "How are pending writes from parallel tasks collected and applied to graph channels?"
            ]
        },
